---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { shortcuts, toolShortcuts, requirements, measured, testMachine } from '../data/facts';
import { release, mac, isReleased, sourcePublic, issuesUrl, websiteRepoUrl, releasesUrl, formatBytes } from '../data/release';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const toc = [
  ['install', 'Install'],
  ['start', 'Getting started'],
  ['clipboard', 'Clipboard'],
  ['export', 'Export and projects'],
  ['shortcuts', 'Keyboard shortcuts'],
  ['troubleshooting', 'Troubleshooting'],
  ['faq', 'Questions'],
  ['platforms', 'Platforms'],
  ['security', 'Security'],
  ['bugs', 'Reporting problems'],
  ['contributing', 'Contributing'],
  ['credits', 'Credits'],
];
---

<Base
  title="CutOut guide — install, use and troubleshoot"
  description="How to install CutOut on a Mac, remove a background, use the clipboard workflow, export, keyboard shortcuts, troubleshooting and verifying downloads."
  path="docs/"
>
  <Prose title="Guide" lede="Everything you need to install CutOut, get a clean cutout and put it to work." toc={toc}>
    <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
    <p>
      CutOut runs on Macs with <strong>Apple silicon</strong> and <strong>{requirements.os}</strong>. The download is a disk image of about 400 MB
      with the Best Quality model inside, so it works offline from the first launch.
    </p>
    {
      isReleased ? (
        <p>
          Download <a href={mac.url}>{mac.file}</a> ({formatBytes(mac.size)}, version {mac.version}) from the <a href={`${base}#get`}>download section</a> or the{' '}
          <a href={mac.releaseUrl} rel="noopener">release page</a>.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p class="note">
          The first public build is being prepared. Once it is published on GitHub Releases, the <a href={`${base}#get`}>download section</a> links to it with its
          size and checksum. The steps below already apply.
        </p>
      )
    }
    <h3 id="first-launch">Opening it the first time</h3>
    <p>
      CutOut 0.1 is signed ad hoc, not with an Apple Developer ID, and it is not notarized by Apple. macOS therefore stops the first launch and asks you to confirm.
      Check the download first (see <a href="#security">Security</a>), then:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the disk image and drag <strong>CutOut</strong> to <strong>Applications</strong>.</li>
      <li>Open CutOut from Applications. macOS reports that it could not verify the app. Choose <strong>Done</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        Open <strong>System Settings ▸ Privacy &amp; Security</strong>. Under <em>Security</em>, CutOut is named with an <strong>Open Anyway</strong> button. Click it,
        confirm with your password or Touch ID, and choose <strong>Open</strong>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      On <strong>macOS 14 Sonoma</strong> there is a shortcut: Control-click CutOut in Applications, choose <strong>Open</strong>, then <strong>Open</strong> again. macOS
      remembers your choice; later launches open normally.
    </p>
    <h3 id="uninstall">Uninstall</h3>
    <p>
      Drag CutOut from Applications to the Bin. To remove its data too, delete <code>~/Library/Application Support/CutOut</code> (downloaded models and the last-session
      backup) and <code>~/Library/Caches/CutOut</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="start">Getting started</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Bring in an image.</strong> Press <kbd>⌘V</kbd> with an image on the clipboard, drop a file on the window, or choose File ▸ Open (<kbd>⌘O</kbd>).</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Remove the background.</strong> Click <em>Remove Background</em> or press <kbd>⌘B</kbd>. Best Quality took about {measured.bestSeconds} seconds per image
        on an M1 in our tests ({measured.firstBestSeconds} s the first time, while the model loads). <kbd>⇧⌘B</kbd> uses Fast mode (Apple Vision, about {measured.fastSeconds} s).
      </li>
      <li><strong>Check it.</strong> Press <kbd>\</kbd> to switch between before and after.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refine if needed.</strong> Show the tools with <kbd>⌥⌘T</kbd>: the <em>Restore</em> brush (<kbd>R</kbd>) brings back anything that was removed, the
        <em>Eraser</em> (<kbd>X</kbd>) and <em>Magic Eraser</em> (<kbd>E</kbd>) remove more, and <em>Refine Edges</em> adjusts radius, smoothing, feather, contrast and
        edge position, with colour decontamination for fringes.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Copy it.</strong> <kbd>⌘C</kbd>, then paste wherever you need it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Removal never deletes pixels: it writes the layer’s mask. <em>Restore All</em> undoes a removal completely, and every step is in Undo (<kbd>⌘Z</kbd>).
    </p>

    <h2 id="clipboard">Clipboard</h2>
    <dl class="defs">
      <dt><kbd>⌘C</kbd> Copy</dt>
      <dd>Puts the result on the clipboard as a PNG with transparency, plus a TIFF that keeps the alpha channel for apps that prefer TIFF.</dd>
      <dt><kbd>⇧⌘C</kbd> Copy &amp; Clear</dt>
      <dd>Copies, then clears the workspace for the next image. If copying fails, the workspace is kept.</dd>
      <dt><kbd>⌥⌘C</kbd> Copy &amp; Close</dt>
      <dd>Copies and quits; the clipboard keeps the image after CutOut has closed.</dd>
      <dt>Copy as JPEG</dt>
      <dd>In the Copy menu. JPEG has no transparency, so CutOut warns before flattening it.</dd>
      <dt><kbd>⌘V</kbd> Paste</dt>
      <dd>Opens the image, or adds it as a new layer if something is already open. <kbd>⌥⌘V</kbd> pastes as a new image instead.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>CutOut reads the clipboard only when you paste. It never monitors it.</p>

    <h2 id="export">Export and projects</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Export PNG</strong> (<kbd>⇧⌘E</kbd>) keeps transparency. <strong>Export JPEG</strong> is in the File menu.</li>
      <li><strong>Save Project</strong> (<kbd>⌘S</kbd>) writes a <code>.cutout</code> file that keeps layers, masks, transforms and text editable.</li>
      <li>CutOut opens PNG, JPEG, HEIC, TIFF, GIF, BMP and WebP. Images over 16,384 px on a side or 120 megapixels are scaled down on import, with a notice.</li>
      <li>If you quit or clear with unsaved work, <strong>File ▸ Restore Last Session</strong> brings it back.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="shortcuts">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption class="visually-hidden">Commands</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Keys</th><th scope="col">Command</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {shortcuts.map(([k, v]) => <tr><td><kbd>{k}</kbd></td><td>{v}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption class="visually-hidden">Tools</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Tool</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {toolShortcuts.map(([k, v]) => <tr><td><kbd>{k}</kbd></td><td>{v}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
    <h3>“Remove Background” opens a model setup window</h3>
    <p>
      The Best Quality model is missing. The full download includes it, so reinstalling that build fixes this. You can also choose <em>Use Fast Mode Now</em> (built in, no
      download) or <em>Install from File…</em> with a CutOut model archive, which is verified before it is installed.
    </p>
    <h3>Best Quality takes 15 seconds or more</h3>
    <p>
      Look at the status bar. <em>Core ML · GPU</em> is the fast path. <em>ONNX Runtime · CPU</em> means CutOut fell back to the slower CPU model (about 14 s and up to
      9 GB of memory on an M1), usually because the Core ML model is missing. Reinstall the full build.
    </p>
    <h3>Glass or a translucent object looks solid</h3>
    <p>
      Segmentation decides what is subject and what isn’t, so it can’t make glass half-transparent. Use the Magic Eraser, Eraser or Refine Edges to finish such areas.
    </p>
    <h3>macOS says CutOut “is damaged”</h3>
    <p>The file was changed or corrupted on the way. Delete it, download it again, and compare its checksum before opening it.</p>

    <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>
    <h3>Is CutOut really free?</h3>
    <p>Yes. No price, trial, subscription, in-app purchase, account or advertising.</p>
    <h3>Does it upload my images?</h3>
    <p>No. Everything happens on your Mac. The app makes no network requests unless you start a model download yourself, and the full download already contains the model.</p>
    <h3>How well does it handle hair, fur — and glass?</h3>
    <p>
      Fur and hair work well; compare them on the home page and zoom in. Truly transparent things such as glass are harder: the model decides what is subject and what
      isn’t, so it can’t make glass half-transparent. Finish those with Refine Edges, the Magic Eraser or the Restore brush.
    </p>
    <h3>What can I paste the result into?</h3>
    <p>Anything that accepts images from the clipboard. Copy puts a PNG with transparency on the clipboard, plus a TIFF that keeps the alpha channel. Copy as JPEG is there too, with a warning, because JPEG can’t keep transparency.</p>
    <h3>Is there a Windows or Intel Mac version?</h3>
    <p>Not yet. The engine is portable Rust so a Windows app can reuse it, but neither a Windows nor an Intel build exists. This site will offer them only when real, tested builds do.</p>

    <h2 id="platforms">Platforms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mac with Apple silicon</strong>, {requirements.os}: supported.</li>
      <li><strong>Intel Mac</strong>: not available. No Intel or Universal build exists yet.</li>
      <li><strong>Windows</strong>: not available. The Rust engine is designed to be reused by a native Windows app, which hasn’t been built.</li>
      <li><strong>Linux</strong>: not available.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Performance figures on this site were measured on an {testMachine}.</p>

    <h2 id="security">Security</h2>
    <h3>Verify your download</h3>
    <p>Every release lists a SHA-256 checksum for each file in <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code>, and this site shows the same value. In Terminal:</p>
    <pre><code>shasum -a 256 ~/Downloads/{mac.file}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The output must match exactly. This website only links to files that are published on GitHub Releases and whose checksum matches both the release’s
      <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code> and GitHub’s own record of the file; otherwise the site isn’t updated.
    </p>
    <h3>What “not notarized” means</h3>
    <p>
      Notarization is Apple’s automated malware scan for apps signed with a paid Developer ID. CutOut 0.1 is signed ad hoc instead, so Apple has not scanned it, and the
      checksum is how you know your copy is the published one. The app runs with the hardened runtime and requests no special permissions (entitlements).
    </p>
    <h3>Reporting a vulnerability</h3>
    <p>
      Please don’t open a public issue. Use GitHub’s private vulnerability reporting on <a href={`${sourcePublic ? release.repo.url : websiteRepoUrl}/security`} rel="noopener">the repository’s Security tab</a>, or see its <code>SECURITY.md</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="bugs">Reporting problems</h2>
    <p>
      Open an issue on <a href={issuesUrl} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>. Include your macOS version, Mac model, CutOut version (CutOut ▸ About CutOut), what you did and what
      happened. Please don’t attach private photos; a screenshot of the window is usually enough.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contributing">Contributing</h2>
    {
      sourcePublic ? (
        <p>
          CutOut’s source is on <a href={release.repo.url} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>. Read its contributing notes before opening a pull request.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          The application’s source code will be published with the first public release. Until then you can help by reporting problems, and this website’s own source is
          open at <a href={websiteRepoUrl} rel="noopener">{websiteRepoUrl.replace('https://', '')}</a>.
        </p>
      )
    }
    {releasesUrl && <p><a href={releasesUrl} rel="noopener">All releases</a></p>}

    <h2 id="credits">Credits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Background removal model: <a href="https://github.com/ZhengPeng7/BiRefNet" rel="noopener">BiRefNet</a> by Peng Zheng et al., MIT licence.</li>
      <li>
        Enamel mug (home page title image): “Enamel mug” by knoe,
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/" rel="license noopener">CC BY 3.0</a>, via
        <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enamel_mug.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>; its cutout, mask, shadow and traced outline
        were made with CutOut.
      </li>
      <li>
        Machined crankset: “Crank Set” by Romantiker,
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" rel="license noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via
        <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crank_Set.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>. The cutout, mask, shadow and traced outline
        made from it with CutOut are shared under the same licence.
      </li>
      <li>
        Golden retriever puppy: “Golden Retriever Puppy in the House” by SapphireDreamPhotography,
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/" rel="license noopener">CC BY 3.0</a>, via
        <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Retriever_Puppy_in_the_House.JPG" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>; its cutout and the app
        screenshots that show it were made with CutOut.
      </li>
      <li>
        Hex bolts: “Hex head bolt” by Susan Wright, public domain, via
        <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hex_head_bolt.tif" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Bolt skyline: “Sechskantschrauben” by Dietmar Rabich,
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" rel="license noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via
        <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sechskantschrauben_--_2020_--_3985-8.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>; the cutout is shared
        under the same licence.
      </li>
      <li>
        Dandelion: “Seed head of a dandelion” by Adesolive, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/" rel="license noopener">CC0</a>, via
        <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seed_head_of_a_dandelion,_specifically_Taraxacum_officinale.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Typeface: <a href="https://github.com/github/mona-sans" rel="noopener">Mona Sans</a> by GitHub, SIL Open Font License 1.1
        (<a href={`${base}licenses/MonaSans-OFL.txt`}>licence text</a>).
      </li>
      <li>Website built with <a href="https://astro.build/" rel="noopener">Astro</a> (MIT).</li>
    </ul>
  </Prose>
</Base>
